<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      §19.2 — the self-managed ConnectionService.

      BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE is held by the platform, not by us: it is
      what stops any app binding this service. Telecom will not use a
      ConnectionService that does not require it.

      There is deliberately NO InCallService here and no dialer intent filter.
      §19.2: Social is never the dialer. NexLink already holds that role, and two
      apps from one developer both claiming it is D1 (§2.2) in its sharpest
      form — the user would be asked to choose between their SMS app and their
      messaging app for the role of "phone".
    -->
    <!--
      §15.3.1 — one permission per declared type, and §15.3.1's warning:
      "Declaring a type 'just in case' is a review liability — declare only what
      is used." So microphone and camera only. mediaProjection arrives with §18
      screen sharing and dataSync with large uploads, each when it is actually
      used, not now.
    -->
    <!--
      §15.4.1 — the RUNTIME permissions behind the service types.
      
      Declaring FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE is not enough: on Android 14+
      startForeground() with type `microphone` throws unless RECORD_AUDIO is
      held AND granted. Measured — without these, all three rungs of §15.2.1's
      fallback chain failed and the service never promoted.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      §17.6.3 — holding RECORD_AUDIO is necessary but NOT sufficient for a
      WebView call.

      Measured on a Galaxy S21: with the mic granted and the foreground service
      running, the first real call still came up silent, and the only evidence
      was two lines from inside Chromium —

          E chromium: audio_manager_android.cc:885 Unable to select
                      communication device!
          I chromium: CONSOLE "Uncaught (in promise) NotReadableError:
                      Could not start audio source"

      WebRTC puts the device into communication mode before opening the input.
      `AudioManager.setCommunicationDevice()` needs MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS, and
      without it the call proceeds to LiveKit, encrypts, and publishes
      m.call.member — everything except audio. The failure is silent at the
      Matrix layer, which is what makes it worth this note.

      Install-time permission: declaring it is the whole fix, no prompt.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CAMERA" />

    <application>
        <!--
          §15.4.1 — runs for the duration of a call only. The declared types are
          the fallback chain's first rung; CallService degrades to microphone and
          then to untyped if an OEM refuses (§15.2.1). A fallback type that is
          not declared here is illegal and throws, which is §15.2.2's second
          invariant — both of these are declared.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".calls.CallService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="microphone|camera" />

        <service
            android:name=".telecom.SocialConnectionService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.telecom.ConnectionService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>
    </application>

</manifest>
